,title,link,text,language,family
0,Hainanese - Wikipedia,https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hainanese,"Hainanese[b] is a variety of Min Chinese spoken in the island of Hainan and regional overseas Chinese communities. In the classification by Yuan Jiahua, it was added to the Southern Min group by him despite being mutually unintelligible with Southern Min varieties such as Hokkien and Teochew. [6]",Hainanese,Sino-Tibetan
1,Hainanese - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hainanese,"Hainanese[b] is a variety of Min Chinese spoken in the island of Hainan and regional overseas Chinese communities. In the classification by Yuan Jiahua, it was added to the Southern Min group by him despite being mutually unintelligible with Southern Min varieties such as Hokkien and Teochew. [6]",Hainanese,Sino-Tibetan
2,Hainan's Intangible Cultural Heritage: Traditional Music and Crafts,https://www.tropicalhainan.com/hainans-intangible-cultural-heritage-traditional-music-and-crafts/,"Li Music Li music is produced using traditional instruments such as the nose organ (鼻箫), the bow flute (口弓)， single wooden drums (独木皮鼓) and the frog gong. The Li possess two primary typed of folk song: those sung in standard Han Chinese or Putonghua and those sung in the Li's own language known as "" Li folk ballads"".",Hainanese,Sino-Tibetan
3,Hainan music in Singapore - Culturepaedia: One-Stop Repository on ...,https://culturepaedia.singaporeccc.org.sg/music/hainan-music-in-singapore/,"These associations played an important role and became centres for Hainanese musicians, who went on to develop and promote Hainanese music in Singapore. Today, Hainanese music generally falls under three categories: folk songs, bayin (instrumental music based on eight instruments), and Hainan opera.",Hainanese,Sino-Tibetan
4,Taoist Music (Hainan Zhai Jiao Ceremonial Music)_Baiduwiki,https://baike.baidu.com/en/item/Taoist+Music+(Hainan+Zhai+Jiao+Ceremonial+Music)/658598,"Hainan Zhai Jiao Ceremonial Music is a significant form of folk music in Hainan Province. It was introduced to Hainan by immigrants from the Jiangnan region, became quite prevalent during the Ming Dynasty, and has been widely circulated throughout various areas of Hainan Province, holding an important position in the spiritual lives of the local people. Wang Honghui, a Ming Dynasty Jinshi (a ...",Hainanese,Sino-Tibetan
5,Hlai Music in Hainan Island | Hlai Artists in Hainan | XY,https://xy3mas93.bandcamp.com/album/hlai-music-in-hainan-island,"Hlai Music in Hainan Island by Hlai Artists in Hainan, released 05 March 2026 1. Nose Flute (Ciong) Welcoming Tune 2. Nose Flute (Ciong) Love Song 3. Ancient Lullaby 4. Love Song Climb Mountains to See You 1 5. Love Song Climb Mountains to See You 2 6. Village Welcoming Song 7. Ancient Tune Crying for Mother 8. Children Song Hen Tune 9. Children Song Insect Tune 10. Love Song My Beloved One 11 ...",Hainanese,Sino-Tibetan
